WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.051
In medieval Cairo, pharmaceutical work did not end

00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:07.135
when the ingredients met. A syrup still had to

00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:10.285
withstand heat, storage, and the possibility of

00:00:10.285 --> 00:00:10.821
failure.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.388
The evidence comes from different places: a

00:00:19.388 --> 00:00:22.014
druggist's handbook, a hospital formulary,

00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:24.702
prescriptions preserved in the Genizah, and

00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:27.703
writings about market misconduct. Each answers a

00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:28.891
different question.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.790
Al-Kūhīn described julep as the common base for

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.815
sweet syrups, using the language of matter and

00:00:38.815 --> 00:00:41.576
form. The fruit gave the base a particular

00:00:41.576 --> 00:00:42.234
character.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.071
The recipe moves through water and sugar, egg-white

00:00:52.071 --> 00:00:55.442
clarification, gentle heating, skimming, straining,

00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:58.813
and a carefully judged final consistency. These are

00:00:58.813 --> 00:01:02.184
practical instructions, not measured modern process

00:01:02.184 --> 00:01:02.779
controls.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.145
Al-Kūhīn even described syrup that had crystallized

00:01:08.145 --> 00:01:11.522
or deteriorated. A measured addition of hot water,

00:01:11.522 --> 00:01:14.900
renewed dissolution, and fruit water could restore

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.251
an acceptable state.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.614
Substitution addressed unavailable or doubtful

00:01:23.614 --> 00:01:26.717
ingredients through perceived therapeutic action,

00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.821
dose, material character, and suitability. It was

00:01:29.821 --> 00:01:32.798
not modern chemical equivalence, and it was not

00:01:32.798 --> 00:01:34.128
permission for fraud.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.646
The Genizah prescription Or.1081 J39 joins an early

00:01:40.646 --> 00:01:43.895
electuary with a later decoction. It shows

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:47.608
therapeutic instructions, but cannot prove every

00:01:47.608 --> 00:01:50.471
ingredient was available in the shop.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.655
This history is not an early version of Good

00:01:55.655 --> 00:01:59.081
Manufacturing Practice. It describes how materials,

00:01:59.081 --> 00:02:01.969
instructions, repair, and trust helped make

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:05.125
medicines recognizable and usable before modern

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.401
analytical testing.
